<article class="env-definition" id="0ADR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0ADR">Definition <span data-tag="0ADR">74.22.11</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $\{ X_ i \to X\} $ be a family of morphisms of algebraic spaces over $S$ with fixed target $X$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Given an algebraic space $U$ over $X$ we have a <em>canonical descent datum</em> on the family of algebraic spaces $X_ i \times _ X U$ by pulling back the trivial descent datum for $U$ relative to $\{ \text{id} : S \to S\} $. We denote this descent datum $(X_ i \times _ X U, can)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A descent datum $(V_ i, \varphi _{ij})$ relative to $\{ X_ i \to S\} $ is called <em>effective</em> if there exists an algebraic space $U$ over $X$ such that $(V_ i, \varphi _{ij})$ is isomorphic to $(X_ i \times _ X U, can)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>